// Original hypothetical demonstration exercises. No invented customer outcomes or performance figures.
const rows = `
crm|customer-profiles|Choose a shopper whose email address is also used by another member of the household. Find the intended person, inspect the available contact details and explain which saved information belongs to them. Then ask a colleague unfamiliar with that customer to repeat the task. This exposes identity ambiguity that a demonstration using one perfect contact can conceal.|A useful result is a record staff can identify and explain. If an important detail is absent, establish whether it was never collected, omitted from an import or unavailable from the connection before deciding how to repair it.
crm|customer-purchase-history|Bring two purchases for one customer: a rose from a previous season and a recent indoor plant. Ask to trace each purchase to its date and available item detail. Then decide which information is relevant to the proposed invitation. The customer may have bought the rose as a gift; a purchase suggests a useful question rather than proving a permanent personal interest.|Judge the history by whether staff can explain its origin and limits. If an item description is abbreviated, inspect its source mapping rather than treating a polished public product name as proof that the transaction has been matched correctly.
crm|customer-activity-timeline|Rehearse a simple sequence using records already available in a test account: an email send, a recorded interaction and a later enquiry. Ask someone to describe the order of events and the next helpful response. Include a second customer who has no tracked activity so staff see how an incomplete timeline differs from an inactive relationship.|Look for a sequence that supports a conversation without overstating intent. A recorded click is a useful signal; it does not establish why the person clicked or whether they completed the action on another website.
crm|customer-csv-imports|Prepare a small file with a normal contact, a missing first name, a phone number with spaces and an address already present in the account. Include a contact with a known opt-out. Review the field mapping before import and inspect the resulting records afterward. Keep the original file so corrections can be checked against a known source.|The sample should preserve identity and known restrictions without silently treating blank or unfamiliar fields as permission. Reconcile the accepted and rejected records before applying the same mapping to the full customer list.
crm|customer-data-cleanup|Use a sample containing two people with the same surname and one person with an outdated email address. Ask what evidence distinguishes a genuine duplicate from a household relationship. Review the correction with someone who knows the source list. The objective is a clearer record, not the largest possible reduction in the contact count.|Record what was changed and preserve access to source evidence. If identity remains uncertain, leaving a record for review is more useful than confidently combining the wrong people and carrying that mistake into every future audience.
crm|customer-list-exports|Start with a small, reviewed audience and state why the export is needed. Open the resulting file in the tool the recipient will actually use. Check the column names, names containing punctuation, phone formats and any required contact preferences. This makes portability a practical test rather than a promise based on the presence of an export button.|The file should be understandable without private knowledge of the CRM. Share only the fields necessary for the task and confirm the destination before passing a customer list to an outside provider.
crm|customer-segments|Write a plain-language audience definition before touching the builder: people who asked about indoor plants and can receive the intended campaign. Choose several known examples on each side of the rule. Compare their saved data with the generated membership, then review the sendable subset. This catches an audience that looks plausible by size but answers the wrong question.|A good segment has an explanation staff can repeat. If the membership surprises you, inspect the actual condition and source field before assuming the total is a reporting problem.
crm|dynamic-segments|Choose one customer who currently qualifies for a lapsed audience and describe the new purchase that should remove them. Introduce the relevant source update through the supported test pathway, then check when membership changes. Review what happens if the update arrives late. This tests the relationship between data freshness and audience rules rather than only the rule editor.|Ask for the expected refresh cadence and a way to recognize stale inputs. Dynamic membership is useful when the team understands when it changes and does not mistake an old snapshot for the present.
crm|static-customer-lists|Create a small group for a particular planning purpose, such as customers who agreed to provide feedback on a workshop idea. Explain who belongs and who can maintain it. Then review a member who later opts out of marketing. A deliberately fixed group can remain useful, but its purpose should not quietly expand into unrestricted promotional permission.|Judge the list by its clear ownership and intended use. Keep the decision to remain in a group separate from the channel rules that determine whether a specific message may be sent.
crm|purchase-based-audiences|Use a product example with two similar names, such as an outdoor rose and a rose-themed gift item. Inspect which recorded field the purchase condition uses and review the matching customers. Then choose a date window that fits the planned invitation. The exercise reveals whether the audience is based on a meaningful item identity or a broad text coincidence.|The team should be able to trace a sample recipient to the relevant transaction. If only category-level data is available, make the invitation broad enough to match what that evidence can actually support.
crm|lapsed-customer-audiences|Compare the store's quiet winter months with a busy spring period. Pick a customer known to have returned recently and check whether that purchase is present in the source. A useful inactivity rule reflects both the business calendar and the coverage of the records being analysed. Without those checks, a missing import can resemble a disappearing relationship.|Begin the campaign with a helpful reason to return, not an accusation that the customer has been absent. Review the underlying dates before describing anyone as lost or declining.
crm|audience-recipient-review|Rehearse the exact moment before a campaign is approved. Select a small named audience, review the displayed eligible number and inspect a few people the team knows. Include one contact who belongs to the group but cannot receive that channel. Ask the reviewer to explain the difference between audience membership and the final sendable recipients.|The approval should identify the intended campaign and audience clearly enough that another staff member could catch a mistaken selection. Stop and resolve an unexplained count before committing the send.
crm|newsletter-builder|Build one realistic newsletter around a single seasonal invitation. Put the useful answer near the top, choose a relevant image and use one primary destination. Then read the message aloud and remove anything that distracts from that purpose. A demonstration becomes more meaningful when staff can revise their own draft rather than only admire a prepared template.|The result should be easy to understand in an inbox and manageable next week. Check the saved draft, audience and links separately; a polished layout is only one part of a ready campaign.
crm|reusable-email-blocks|Prepare a reusable visiting-information section that includes the store address and a link to current hours. Add it to a second campaign with a different main topic. Review which information remains fixed and which needs updating. This helps the team decide whether a section deserves reuse or should remain specific to the original campaign.|Reuse should reduce repeated editing without hiding maintenance. Give someone responsibility for reviewing recurring details, especially seasonal hours, phone numbers and destinations that can change independently of the saved block.
crm|personalized-email-greetings|Preview a normal first name, a missing name and an imported value that contains a full household description. Read the greeting in the context of the complete sentence. Then send a test using the supported preview pathway. This exposes awkward punctuation and fallback behaviour that can be invisible when testing only a single well-formed customer.|The greeting should feel natural in each reviewed case. Fix incorrect source values or choose a safer greeting rather than relying on personalization to make poor contact data look personal.
crm|email-image-blocks|Use a real workshop photograph with an important subject near one edge. Add the event title and booking information as readable text beside or below it, then inspect the mobile presentation. Review the email with images unavailable as well. The invitation should still explain its purpose and provide a usable next step.|A useful image supports the message rather than carrying all of it. Check that the photograph represents the actual offer and that its crop does not remove the detail customers need to understand what they are booking.
crm|mobile-email-previews|Use the longest realistic heading, a multi-line button label and an image with a difficult crop. Check the order in which a phone reader encounters those elements. Then send to an inbox the team actually uses and compare the result with the preview. The goal is to catch practical reading problems before the campaign reaches customers.|The primary action should be obvious and usable without zooming. If an email client changes the layout, simplify the affected section and test again rather than assuming every recipient sees the editor's canvas.
crm|test-email-sends|Make the test reviewer responsible for a complete path: sender name, subject, greeting, image, main link, secondary links and footer. Open the destination pages as a customer would see them, without relying on an administrator's signed-in view. Record the corrections in the draft and perform another test only when a material change needs confirmation.|The final approval should reflect the actual version that will be sent. A test from yesterday does not verify a link, date or content section changed this morning.
crm|scheduled-email-campaigns|Schedule a test campaign around a specific local date, then inspect the saved schedule in the account. Include an event whose booking closes before the session begins so the timing distinction is explicit. Ask what happens if the team edits the campaign afterward or discovers the event page is not ready. The scheduling discussion should include ownership of the last review.|A useful schedule makes the intended time easy to confirm. Keep a clear process for correcting or cancelling a queued campaign when the underlying offer changes.
crm|campaign-drafts|Start a campaign with a recognizable working name and save it before every section is complete. Reopen it through the normal campaign list and check the content that persisted. Then have the next reviewer identify what remains to be done. This tests whether the draft can support real work across interruptions rather than only a single editing session.|A useful draft has a clear purpose and an obvious review step. Do not let a saved state imply that the audience, sender or current offer has already been approved.
crm|sender-readiness-checks|Walk through the account's current sender state and identify the exact requirement shown by the product. If a DNS or identity step is required, establish who can change that service and how readiness will be rechecked. Distinguish the account setup from the later delivery report. This prevents the team from interpreting every sending issue as an editor problem.|The reviewer should leave knowing which requirement is resolved, which remains and who owns the next step. Avoid treating a successful test message as proof that all future campaigns will reach every inbox.
crm|seasonal-marketing-suggestions|Take one suggested theme and compare it with the store's actual stock and local timing. For example, a spring-care idea may need different wording in a colder location. Ask the person responsible for marketing to adapt the suggestion into a useful invitation with a real destination. The idea should shorten preparation while leaving editorial judgement with the business.|Judge the suggestion by whether it helps the team produce something relevant. Confirm dates, availability and factual advice before using the draft in a public campaign.
crm|customer-journey-builder|Sketch a small journey on paper first: the event that qualifies someone, the message they should receive and the reason for any wait. Recreate that sequence using available test data, then ask how someone avoids receiving the same welcome twice. Review a customer who cannot receive the chosen channel so the exception is part of the demonstration.|The team should understand what starts, advances and stops the journey. A visually complete sequence is not ready until the required source event and recipient eligibility have been verified.
crm|welcome-email-sequences|Write the first message as an introduction to the actual store: what subscribers will hear about, how often and which useful resource they can visit now. Keep the second step purposeful instead of repeating the first offer. Rehearse with a new eligible test subscriber and review the recorded sequence before inviting the full audience.|The welcome should set an expectation the team can maintain. Revisit the sequence when hours, departments or recurring events change so new subscribers do not begin with outdated information.
crm|sms-campaign-builder|Draft a short invitation with the store identity and one clear destination. Read it on a phone and inspect the linked page at the same screen size. Then review why the chosen audience is eligible for SMS. This keeps the campaign focused on a useful customer action rather than treating a text as a shorter version of the whole newsletter.|The message should make sense without extra explanation from staff. Review usage implications and any sender requirements in the actual proposed setup before relying on a larger public campaign.
crm|scheduled-sms-messages|Use a deadline with a clear local time, such as the final day to book a workshop. Decide when a reminder remains helpful and when it becomes an interruption. Review the saved schedule and the destination's closing state. A useful scheduling demonstration includes the case where the event is cancelled or sold out before the text is due.|Assign responsibility for checking that the message is still appropriate before release. Timing is part of the customer experience, not merely a calendar setting.
crm|sms-consent-handling|Review three sample contacts: one with documented SMS permission, one subscribed only to email and one with an uncertain imported phone number. Ask which people can enter the text campaign and why. Follow the uncertain case back to its collection source. This tests the practical distinction between having a number and having the right to use that channel.|The result should be understandable from the saved evidence. When permission is missing or unclear, choose an appropriate alternative route rather than changing the field simply to increase campaign reach.
crm|sms-opt-out-protection|Use an authorized test number to rehearse the configured opt-out pathway, then inspect the resulting contact state. Review whether that number remains in a group while being excluded from sending. Also discuss a queued message or journey step so staff understand that an older audience choice should not override a later request to stop.|The useful evidence is the suppression state and the handling of later sending decisions. Do not make the test about sending promotional messages to a real customer who has already opted out.
crm|sms-delivery-reporting|Review a test campaign with distinct submitted, delivered or failed outcomes where the configured provider supplies them. Pick one unsuccessful recipient and ask what the available record says. Keep the explanation tied to the provider feedback instead of guessing whether the person read or ignored the message. A useful report should guide the next support question.|The team should distinguish an accepted sending request from confirmation supplied later in the process. If the status has not resolved, record that uncertainty rather than labelling it a successful delivery.
crm|sms-link-tracking|Choose a single workshop destination and open the test link from the message. Inspect the recorded interaction separately from any booking record. Discuss repeated clicks and automated scanners as possible reasons a count may differ from the number of interested people. This keeps the report useful without turning a link interaction into an unsupported sales claim.|A useful next step is to compare the campaign activity with the actual desired outcome. Confirm the destination works before using click totals to judge the message itself.
crm|customer-intelligence|Begin with a question the owner already asks, such as which previous customers may appreciate a timely invitation. Review the available reporting period and inspect the explanation behind a suggested relationship. Then open the supporting customer history. The exercise should connect a summary insight to evidence and a sensible next action that staff can take.|Judge the tool by whether it improves a real decision. If the recommendation cannot be explained using known records, investigate coverage or assumptions before converting it into a campaign.
crm|customer-spending-insights|Compare two customers with different patterns: one larger purchase long ago and several smaller recent purchases from another. Choose the same reporting window and inspect the available transaction detail. Discuss how the store would respond differently to each relationship. This prevents a single lifetime total from becoming the only definition of a valuable customer.|The reported amount should have an understandable period and source. Use spending as context for helpful service rather than assuming it describes a person's future value or willingness to buy.
crm|campaign-click-reporting|Use a newsletter with separate destinations for a workshop, plant collection and visiting information. Review interactions by destination and identify the question each link helps answer. A visit to hours may signal planning, while a booking-page click suggests a different next step. Check the actual downstream records before deciding which activity resulted in a sale.|A useful report changes the next editorial decision without overstating certainty. If an apparent zero conflicts with known activity, inspect the tracked destination and reporting path rather than dismissing the customer evidence.
crm|campaign-recipient-reports|Pick a campaign and a recipient the team can identify. Follow the recorded outcome through the report, noting whether it describes eligibility, sending, provider acceptance or delivery. Then consider what a helpful response to the customer's enquiry would be. This gives support a repeatable process rather than a choice between resending blindly and assuming the customer overlooked the message.|The reviewer should be able to state exactly what the evidence confirms. Preserve the distinction between a message record and the customer's actual inbox or reading behaviour.
crm|comparable-sales-periods|Choose matching spring date windows and inspect whether both sources cover the full interval. Include any store closure or missing import that changes the trading context. Then compare the result with a deliberately mismatched full-year versus partial-year view. Seeing the contrast helps the owner understand why a visually dramatic percentage can still be a poor decision aid.|The comparison should state its period and meaningful limitations. Prefer an honest incomplete result over a precise-looking growth figure built from unequal coverage.
crm|pos-data-connections|Bring a sample customer, a dated transaction and an item line from the current POS or approved export. Ask which of those records the supported pathway supplies and how a later update is recognized. Review the last successful refresh and a missing-field case. This tests the useful data contract instead of relying on a familiar provider logo.|The store should know the update cadence, historical scope and person responsible for exceptions. Keep manual import steps visible when they remain part of the operating process.
website|page-content-editor|Choose an ordinary correction that staff make regularly, such as changing a department introduction or adding a new service detail. Make the edit using the permissions the responsible person will actually have. Save it, revisit the page and review the public result in a separate visitor session. This checks the whole publishing path rather than just the typing experience.|The owner should know which changes they can make confidently and where assistance is appropriate. Keep structural redesign and routine text corrections distinct when discussing training and support.
website|homepage-sections|Sketch the main customer journeys before arranging the page: discovering plants, finding an event, planning a visit and asking for advice. Give each section a reason to exist in that sequence. Then remove one section and ask whether the page becomes clearer. A useful homepage is often the result of choosing what deserves attention rather than adding every available content type.|Judge the page by the ease of the next decision. If every section asks for a different action with equal urgency, revise the hierarchy before polishing decorative details.
website|landing-pages|Use a specific campaign, such as a winter workshop advertised in a newsletter. Write down exactly what the invitation promises, then inspect whether the landing page answers that promise immediately. Include the date, location, price or approved enquiry process as appropriate. Review the page without the surrounding email so it still makes sense when someone shares the address separately.|The destination should stand on its own and provide a clear next step. Avoid making visitors repeat the search they already completed when they chose to click the campaign.
website|about-pages|Collect a small set of approved facts about the business and photographs of the people customers will meet. Build the page around those real details, then ask someone new to the area what they learned. The useful test is whether the page explains the store's character and service clearly without relying on generic claims that could describe any retailer.|Keep the story grounded in facts the team is comfortable publishing. A shorter authentic introduction usually gives customers more confidence than an elaborate biography with uncertain details.
website|department-pages|Choose two departments with different customer needs, such as indoor plants and landscape supplies. Compare the questions a visitor should be able to answer on each page. Use a consistent visual system while allowing the information and next action to reflect the department. Then test how a customer reaches it from the header and returns to related content.|The department should feel like part of the same business while serving its own audience. Avoid forcing every page into identical copy simply because the layout is reusable.
website|service-pages|Take one actual service enquiry and identify the details staff repeatedly have to clarify: location, scope, timing and what happens next. Put the approved information on the page in a useful order. Then ask someone to describe what they would expect after contacting the store. This reveals vague wording that attractive photography can hide.|The page should prepare an appropriate enquiry and reduce avoidable confusion. Do not publish an instant quote or guaranteed service window unless the business actually supports that commitment.
website|brand-styling|Review a small reference set before applying the design broadly: the homepage, one article, a department page and the mobile header. Use the actual approved logo and real content lengths. Decide which elements remain consistent and where variation is useful. This produces a practical visual direction that can survive ordinary editing rather than a single polished presentation screen.|A useful brand system makes the next page easier to create. If a design only works with a particular heading length or unusually perfect photograph, refine it before expanding the site.
website|logo-presentation|Test the real logo in its intended header position using both a wide desktop and a narrow phone. Check clear space, contrast and the relationship with navigation controls. Include the open mobile menu so the review covers the busy state as well as the clean first impression. A logo that looks good on an isolated canvas may need a different header arrangement.|Preserve the approved mark and give it a suitable layout. Do not solve crowding by shrinking the logo until the business name becomes unreadable.
website|hero-images|Bring a photograph with a clear subject and examine it in the actual banner proportions. Add the real headline and supporting text before approving the composition. Review the phone crop and the visible contrast at the places where the text falls. The image should express the experience of the store while leaving the invitation easy to read.|Choose another photograph when the subject and text cannot coexist comfortably. A darker overlay should not become the default answer to every difficult image.
website|image-focal-points|Use a tall Christmas tree photograph and review it in a wide banner and a narrow card. Identify the part of the subject that must remain visible, then test the available focal controls in each context. Check the final result after saving. This exposes the difference between selecting a focal point and assuming the entire original photograph will always fit.|The meaningful result is a useful composition in the actual display shape. If preserving the subject requires too much empty space, choose a second crop or a better-proportioned source image.
website|content-image-galleries|Select a small image set that answers a particular question, such as what a workshop project looks like from several angles. Remove repeated views and place the clearest image first. Review the sequence and any captions on a phone. This helps the gallery contribute information rather than making the visitor scroll through a collection assembled only because the images were available.|Each photograph should earn its place. Check loading, crop and permission, and keep essential booking or service details in readable page text rather than relying on a caption alone.
website|readable-page-layouts|Use an actual long service explanation, not a short sample paragraph. Divide it around the questions a customer is likely to scan for and review the reading width on a large monitor. Then read the same content on a phone with ordinary browser settings. Check headings, links and the space between sections as part of one reading experience.|The page should remain comfortable with the content the business really needs to publish. Avoid cutting essential information solely to make a rigid layout appear tidy.
website|website-navigation|Write down the most common visitor tasks and ask someone outside the team which menu labels they would choose. Test those paths on the website, including the destination page and the route back. This often reveals labels that make sense internally but do not help a new customer, such as an overly broad Resources link hiding events and visiting information.|The result should be predictable destinations rather than a large number of links. Keep the customer's vocabulary at the centre of the decision.
website|dropdown-menus|Group a small set of related pages, then rehearse the menu with a mouse, touch and keyboard. Include a long page label and check that every destination remains readable. Review what happens when someone opens a second menu or moves away. A compact dropdown should simplify the header without becoming a hidden second navigation system that only a practiced user understands.|Use grouping where it clarifies a relationship. If the menu needs several nested levels to make sense, revisit the site's page organization before adding more interaction.
website|mobile-website-navigation|Begin with a practical task such as finding directions or a workshop date. Hold the phone in the way a visitor would and follow the path without using a desktop hover interaction. Open the menu, choose the destination and inspect the resulting page. Review long labels and the business logo together so a small-screen layout does not trade recognition for a crowded control bar.|A useful result has readable labels and comfortable touch targets with no sideways scrolling. Test the open state as carefully as the page underneath it.
website|contact-pages|Choose an enquiry staff receive frequently and decide which contact route serves it best. Review the page's explanation, fields and destination using a controlled test. If a form is involved, confirm where the record or notification appears. A visible success message is only one part of the path; the receiving team must be able to act on the enquiry.|Set an expectation that matches real staffing. Keep urgent questions directed toward a route the business actually monitors during the stated hours.
website|store-visit-information|Ask someone unfamiliar with the location to plan a visit using only the website. Have them identify the address, current hours, entrance and any relevant pickup guidance. Follow the map link and compare it with the actual arrival point. This is a practical way to discover that accurate information can still be difficult to find or easy to misunderstand.|The page should reduce uncertainty before someone leaves home. Assign ownership of holiday changes so hours do not remain correct everywhere except the one page visitors use most.
website|connected-page-links|Start with a useful article and ask what question the reader is likely to have next. Add a small number of descriptive links to those destinations, then inspect the route as a visitor. A care article might lead to a related workshop or department, but the connection should reflect the content rather than forcing a sales button into every paragraph.|The link should explain what the reader will find. Review old campaign and article links when destination pages are renamed or seasonal content changes.
website|seasonal-homepage-modes|Prepare a real seasonal transition, such as autumn planting to Christmas trees. Review the selected imagery, headline, featured destinations and store operating details together. Inspect the next version as a customer, then identify the process for returning to the normal seasonal plan. This turns a seasonal mode into an understandable publishing decision rather than a decorative theme switch.|The homepage should reflect what the store is actually offering at that time. Product availability, event publication and operating hours still need their own review.
website|scheduled-seasonal-changes|Use explicit start and end dates and ask how they relate to the site's configured time handling. Prepare a second season close to the first so boundary behaviour can be explained. Review what happens if the owner applies an exception before the scheduled transition. The objective is a schedule the business can reason about when a season starts early or ends late.|The responsible person should know which version is expected to appear and how to inspect it. A date entered in a form is not enough evidence that all linked content is ready.
website|seasonal-homepage-previews|Open the next seasonal version and inspect it with the staff who will maintain it. Use their real photographs, category destinations and planned dates. Ask the reviewer to find the main invitation and explain the next step. Then check the current public version separately so approval does not depend on remembering which screen is preview and which is live.|A preview should make a future change easier to assess. Confirm the published selection after activation rather than assuming that approval of the preview completed the release.
website|seasonal-content-overrides|Rehearse an exception with a clear reason and end condition, such as delaying the spring opening announcement. Identify the normal seasonal plan, apply the intended override and review which content is visible. Then explain how the business returns to the scheduled sequence. The useful exercise includes removing the exception, not just creating it.|The owner should understand the effect and duration of the override. Keep temporary decisions visible so they do not quietly block the next season's prepared content.
website|featured-seasonal-products|Choose a small seasonal selection and check the public destination for every featured item or category. Use the actual photographs and review their crops on the homepage. Then inspect the associated product information separately. A homepage can be visually correct while the linked category is empty, unavailable or still carrying content from the previous season.|The feature should help visitors find something useful now. Keep merchandising approval distinct from the checks that make an item purchasable online.
website|holiday-opening-notices|Write an exact holiday example with a closure date and reopening date. Place the notice where someone planning a visit will encounter it, then compare the contact page, footer and other visible hours. Read the wording after the holiday to identify what must be removed. This prevents an important temporary message from becoming confusing permanent content.|The published website should tell one consistent story about the visit. External map listings and social accounts need their own updates because a page edit does not automatically change them.
website|garden-advice-articles|Choose a question the sales team answered several times this week. Write the explanation using the plants, seasons and conditions relevant to the store, then ask a knowledgeable colleague to review it. Add a clear title and a useful related destination. The article should be something staff are comfortable sharing directly with a customer who needs the answer.|Judge the content by its accuracy and usefulness before judging its length. A specific helpful explanation is more valuable than a broad article assembled around repeated search terms.
website|blog-topic-organization|Review a sample of existing articles and group them according to the questions readers bring: indoor plants, outdoor care, seasonal projects or store news. Choose labels the public will understand and test them against an article that could fit in more than one group. Then browse the archive on a phone to see whether the structure helps someone recover an older answer.|A useful topic system stays consistent as new articles are added. Avoid creating categories that contain only an outdated announcement or duplicate another topic's purpose.
website|page-titles-and-descriptions|Choose a department page and an advice article that currently share a generic title. Write a distinct purpose for each before editing the metadata. Review the published output and make sure it reflects the visible content. This is a clearer test than filling every title with the business name and every gardening phrase the team can think of.|The title should help a person choose the right result. Keep promises modest: useful metadata improves clarity but cannot guarantee the text a search engine displays or the position it assigns.
website|search-friendly-page-addresses|Create a descriptive address for a new page, then examine a proposed change to an established one. Identify where the old address may already exist in newsletters, printed material or external links. Review the supported redirect plan before changing it. This makes the address a durable part of the customer journey rather than a cosmetic field edited whenever the headline changes.|Choose a path that remains understandable over time. Avoid unnecessary date-specific or campaign-specific changes when the page is intended to serve an ongoing purpose.
website|published-content-sitemaps|Inspect the sitemap for the website's actual domain and find a few known published pages. Include an article or product beyond the first visible listing page so the review considers a growing site. Open the listed destinations and check that they are appropriate public content. A well-formed XML file is useful only if its addresses reflect the intended website.|Sitemap discovery and search indexing are separate outcomes. Keep unpublished, private or incomplete content out of the public discovery path and review important omissions before assuming a search engine is responsible.
website|website-redirect-planning|Collect several valuable old addresses: a widely shared article, a department and a seasonal page. Choose the closest useful new destination for each and test the response from the old address. Include one typo or missing page to see how the site handles an unresolved destination. A redirect plan should preserve meaning instead of sending every visitor to the homepage.|The result should help someone with an old bookmark continue their task. Check for loops and keep access to the mapping so future editors understand why a rule exists.
website|event-information-pages|Prepare a workshop page using the actual project image, date, duration, location and approved inclusions. Ask a person unfamiliar with the event to explain what they would be attending and what they need to bring. Then follow the configured booking or enquiry route. This checks whether the invitation and the operational next step tell the same story.|A useful event page prevents surprises. Keep published information synchronized with the actual session and review sold-out, postponed or cancelled states when those become relevant.
website|workshop-promotion-links|Use a newsletter that mentions two sessions with similar names. Copy each public destination into the intended invitation and test the links without administrator access. Check the specific session, date and booking state after arriving. This catches a common problem: a technically valid link that sends someone to a general archive or the wrong occurrence of an event.|The customer should arrive at the invitation they chose. Recheck promoted links when an event is rescheduled or a new session is added under a similar title.
website|fundraising-organization-pages|Create a demonstration with two participating organizations in the same seasonal campaign. Open each group's public destination and compare the identity, campaign invitation and supporter path. Then follow the journey far enough to confirm that the selected group remains clear. This makes organization context part of the review rather than assuming the logo at the top of the page is sufficient.|Supporters should understand who benefits before they commit. Keep private coordinator information separate from the public story and confirm campaign publication independently.
website|fundraiser-application-pages|Submit a controlled example using the fields the store actually needs to review an application. Inspect the applicant's visible next step and the receiving destination on the staff side. Include the correct business contact so the review does not depend on a personal inbox nobody monitors. The application should begin a manageable process, not create the impression of instant approval.|The store should know who reviews the request and what information is still required. Keep the distinction between interest, approval and a published sellable campaign explicit.
website|gift-registry-pages|Prepare a simple occasion with a short introduction and a few recognizable items. Review the chosen visibility setting and open the guest route separately from the registrant's editing view. Ask a guest to describe how participation works using the configured experience. This reveals whether the page explains the difference between viewing a wish list, claiming an item and completing a purchase.|The registry should make the occasion understandable without exposing private details unintentionally. Confirm the actual claiming and payment pathway before describing an item as purchased.
website|newsletter-signup-journeys|Choose a useful article and add a signup invitation that explains what the subscriber will receive. Complete a controlled test and inspect the configured destination, including the relevant contact preferences. Then review the confirmation experience as a visitor. This tests the relationship between public content and the receiving audience rather than only whether the form displays an animation.|The business should be able to locate the test signup and explain the recorded permission. A visible form is not proof that the intended CRM account or audience received the record.

ecomm|plant-product-pages|Choose a plant with a specific cultivar and container size. Review the public name, description, photograph and available options, then ask someone unfamiliar with the catalogue to explain what they would receive. Compare that explanation with the staff item record. This makes the listing a test of shared understanding rather than a design approval based only on an attractive photograph.|The customer and the person preparing the order should identify the same item. If either relies on an ambiguous name, improve the information before inviting purchases.
ecomm|product-size-variants|Use two sizes of the same plant with different prices and stock identities. Select each option on the public page and follow it into the cart and supported test checkout. Inspect the accepted order afterward. This is where a small mapping error becomes visible: a correct plant name can still be attached to the wrong container, price or source quantity.|The selected size should remain clear throughout the journey. Verify the actual variant mapping rather than assuming two similar labels refer to the same physical stock.
ecomm|common-and-botanical-names|Bring a source record with an abbreviated botanical name and a common name customers recognize. Review the proposed public presentation with someone who can verify the plant identity. Include a second plant with a similar common name to expose ambiguity. The exercise should preserve useful internal identifiers while making the public listing easier for a gardener to understand.|A cleaner name is valuable only when it still refers to the right plant. Keep cultivar and source evidence available when reviewing a proposed match.
ecomm|product-photography|Inspect a product that appears in a category card, a detail gallery and a featured homepage selection. Check the actual image in each shape and confirm whether it is exact or representative. Wait for images to finish loading before judging the result. This catches an incorrect crop or a silent fallback that can be missed when reviewing only the original uploaded file.|The final public page should show a useful and accurate photograph, not merely have a nonempty image field. Review licensing and representative-image wording alongside visual quality.
ecomm|plant-care-descriptions|Use a plant whose light, moisture or mature-size information matters to the decision. Read the public care section as a beginner and compare it with the verified reference. Remove internal stock codes and operational information from the advice. Then ask whether the description helps someone decide if the plant fits their space and ability to care for it.|The information should be specific enough to help without promising a guaranteed outcome. Local conditions and the actual plant identity still deserve knowledgeable review.
ecomm|master-catalogue-matching|Choose one clear match and one ambiguous source item. Review the identifiers, botanical information and container evidence used for each proposal. Ask what remains unresolved in the ambiguous case and how an approved decision is retained. This tests the value of a matching process that can say it needs review rather than making every imported row appear complete.|The result should preserve source identity and make uncertainty visible. A successful match is a content relationship; it is not proof of current availability or authorization to publish a sellable offer.
ecomm|product-category-browsing|Ask a shopper to find a useful group without naming a specific plant. Review whether category labels follow their language and whether the items inside match that expectation. Include an empty or off-season category in the discussion. A category earns its place by making a decision easier, not because the inventory system happens to contain the same label.|Public browsing should help people move toward a sensible choice. Keep internal accounting or supplier groupings out of customer navigation when they do not serve that purpose.
ecomm|shop-product-search|Test several realistic queries: a complete plant name, part of the name and the way customers commonly ask for it in the store. Review the result information and open the intended listing. Include an item that is not publicly available so staff can explain what a search result does and does not represent. This is more useful than testing only the exact catalogue title.|A helpful result should be recognizable and lead to the correct listing. Missing search results need investigation of publication and naming before being interpreted as a stock statement.
ecomm|product-filters|Choose a broad category and apply two available criteria that a shopper would reasonably combine. Inspect the matches, then deliberately choose a combination that returns nothing. Review how to remove a condition or start again. This tests whether filtering helps someone recover and learn, rather than leading them into an empty page with no understandable next step.|The available attributes must be consistent enough to support the filter. If a meaningful product is excluded because its data is missing, correct that coverage before judging customer demand.
ecomm|featured-product-collections|Build a small example around a clear purpose, such as plants for autumn containers. Review the selected listings as a group and ask whether the names, images and care information make the relationship obvious. Follow the public collection link from a test campaign. The selection should still be useful when someone lands there without reading the original newsletter.|The collection needs editorial and operational review. Remove products that undermine the promise through missing information or unavailable buying options, even if they look attractive in isolation.
ecomm|catalogue-only-storefronts|Review the site as a visitor who wants to research a purchase before coming in. Open a listing, inspect the available information and find the intended next step. Then check that the experience does not accidentally imply that an unfinished payment path is ready. A browse-first store can be useful when its purpose is clear and the information is maintained.|Customers should understand whether they are researching or ordering. Keep the decision to enable commerce separate from the work of publishing a helpful catalogue.
ecomm|publication-quality-review|Take a small imported batch containing a complete plant, a missing image and an uncertain variant match. Review each item's readiness and explain what must be corrected. Include the store's online-ordering state as a separate part of the discussion. This makes publication a deliberate quality decision instead of assuming that every row accepted by an importer belongs in front of shoppers.|The approved range should be explainable item by item. Preserve review-needed states rather than hiding uncertainty with a generic photograph or a plausible public name.
ecomm|shopping-cart-review|Create a representative basket with two sizes of one product and a second item using a different available receiving arrangement. Review the selected details and quantities before proceeding. Ask the customer to explain what will happen next, then compare that understanding with the supported order path. This catches confusion that can survive a technically successful add-to-cart action.|The basket should make the customer's actual choice clear. Where combinations are restricted, the customer needs an understandable way to adjust the selection before payment.
ecomm|checkout-price-validation|Rehearse a price update in an appropriate test setup while an older cart remains open. Follow the enabled checkout path and inspect the final amount and accepted order. Discuss the expected customer message when the available offer has changed. This gives the team a concrete way to verify authoritative pricing rather than trusting the amount displayed earlier in the browser.|The customer-facing total and the saved payment and order should reconcile. Resolve any unexplained difference before the product is offered for real purchases.
ecomm|online-payment-setup|Review the actual merchant account and the readiness state required by the proposed checkout. Use an authorized test purchase with a real product configuration and inspect the resulting record. Include the case where a required setup step is incomplete so staff know how the store behaves before activation. A visually complete catalogue does not establish payment readiness.|The person responsible for launch should know what has been verified and which account owns the payment relationship. Keep test success, live activation and settlement expectations distinct.
ecomm|checkout-tax-readiness|Use representative eligible addresses and products in the configured test pathway. Review the calculation shown to the customer and the saved amounts on the accepted order. Ask what happens when required calculation setup is missing or unavailable. The demonstration should establish the product's behaviour without substituting for the business's review of its applicable obligations and registrations.|The result should make readiness and failure handling understandable. Do not work around a blocked required calculation by presenting an unverified amount as final.
ecomm|order-confirmations|Complete a controlled order and inspect the confirmation the customer actually receives through the configured pathway. Compare the item, variant, amount and receiving information with the accepted order. Then read it as someone who has never collected from the store before. This is a practical way to find a message that confirms payment but leaves the next step unclear.|The confirmation should describe the saved purchase accurately and explain the immediate next action. Verify delivery evidence separately from the fact that the order record exists.
ecomm|checkout-stock-safeguards|Use a test item with a small available quantity and rehearse the supported changing-stock case. Review when the checkout rechecks or reserves availability and what happens if the source is stale. Inspect the rejected path as carefully as the successful one. The useful claim is the behaviour of the enabled configuration under a defined condition, not a general guarantee that overselling can never occur.|The store should know which quantity is authoritative and how an unavailable item is handled. Do not infer the active checkout engine from the appearance of a modern product page.
ecomm|store-pickup-options|Take one paid test order from product selection to staff preparation. Review the pickup location and instructions shown before payment, on the accepted order and in any configured customer message. Ask the person handling collection whether those details are enough to prepare and hand over the items. This connects the public promise to the physical work in the store.|A useful pickup flow has a clear location and an understandable readiness process. Avoid equating order acceptance with an immediate promise that the plants are already prepared.
ecomm|local-delivery-information|Use one address inside the approved service area and another just outside it. Review the delivery option, applicable charge and customer explanation for both. Include a product that creates a practical delivery exception if the store's rules support one. This tests the relationship between the service the business can provide and the option the website presents.|The staff and customer should agree on what delivery includes. Review operational capacity and exceptions before publishing a service promise based only on a successful address entry.
ecomm|product-receiving-options|Compare a small plant with a bulky container using the store's actual configured methods. Inspect the options on each product and follow the selected one into the cart. Ask why a method is available for one and not the other. This gives the team a useful test of product-specific rules instead of assuming a store-level delivery label covers every item.|The available choice should be supported for the actual product, source and destination. Keep the customer explanation simple while confirming the underlying arrangement with staff.
ecomm|shipping-readiness-review|Choose a proposed ship-to-home product and identify its packaging, eligible destinations and actual rate source. Rehearse a supported order and a deliberate exception in the appropriate environment. Review who prepares the shipment and resolves a delivery problem. This reveals the operational work that a shipping option on a product page cannot establish by itself.|Only describe a shipping offer as ready when its eligibility, rates and fulfilment process have been verified. A benchmark calculation should remain clearly distinguished from a live carrier quote.
ecomm|order-fulfilment-records|Open an accepted order after changing the public product page in a test setup. Review which receiving details and item information remain attached to the original purchase. Ask the staff member preparing it to identify the next supported action. This checks whether the work follows the customer's saved agreement rather than content that may now describe a different offer.|The record should be sufficient to explain what the store still needs to do. Review order-type differences instead of assuming every campaign or ticket follows an ordinary product fulfilment path.
ecomm|pickup-instruction-content|Write instructions for an unfamiliar collection point, including the location, relevant window and what the customer should bring. Review them with someone who has not attended before. Then inspect the saved order and configured message to see whether those details remain available after purchase. The wording should reduce the need for a customer to call the store for basic arrival information.|The instructions should describe an agreed arrangement, not create one accidentally. Review campaign-specific and ordinary-store collection details independently when both are offered.
ecomm|fundraising-campaigns|Prepare a demonstration campaign with a small approved product range and two participating groups. Review the dates, contribution terms and public destinations, then follow one supporter through the configured purchase path. Inspect how the organization and campaign remain attached to the accepted order. This tests the whole program rather than only the attractiveness of its landing page.|The supporter should know who benefits, and staff should be able to trace that choice afterward. Keep campaign publication and selling readiness separate from initial organization setup.
ecomm|fundraising-contribution-terms|Use a simple fixed-contribution item and a percentage-contribution item. Walk through a discounted example and a supported item refund, keeping merchandise, tax and delivery amounts distinct. Review the resulting organization earnings with the person responsible for reconciliation. The calculation should be understandable before the campaign opens, not discovered only when the group asks for its payment.|The terms should survive ordinary corrections without becoming ambiguous. Confirm how payments to the group are recorded, and do not confuse a ledger entry with an automatic transfer of funds.
ecomm|fundraising-reports|Review a small campaign example containing an accepted order, a supported refund and a recorded organization payment. Filter to the intended group and reconcile the remaining balance to those records. Open or export the relevant detail where supported. This makes the report a practical explanation of the obligation rather than a total that only the original operator can interpret.|The person reviewing the balance should understand what is included and what remains unresolved. Keep payment recording and actual movement of money separate in both training and customer-facing explanations.
ecomm|workshop-ticketing|Use the actual session details, ticket type and available place configuration in a controlled demonstration. Complete the enabled test purchase, then ask the event team to find the booking and explain the attendee's next step. Review a session that is unavailable as well. This tests the connection between the invitation, payment path and staff record without assuming every event type behaves identically.|A successful ordinary product checkout does not prove paid ticketing. Verify the exact session path, including any separate capacity, cart or confirmation behaviour required by the proposed setup.
ecomm|free-event-registration|Prepare a free session using only the attendee details the team needs. Complete a controlled registration and inspect the confirmation and staff record through the available workflow. Ask the person running the event to locate the attendee without help from the person who created the page. This exposes a form that collects information but does not produce a useful operational handoff.|The event team should understand how registrations relate to capacity and attendance. Keep free registration distinct from payment evidence and avoid promising a place beyond the configured rules.
ecomm|registry-product-lists|Create a short list with recognizable products and requested quantities. Review the guest journey using the configured visibility and claiming or purchasing path, then return to the registrant's view. Ask how a partial claim or changed product would be understood. This makes the list a shared expectation between host, guest and store rather than assuming the presence of an item means a completed sale.|The terms requested, claimed and purchased should have distinct meanings in the demonstrated setup. Confirm the actual payment relationship before relying on the list to prove an order.
ecomm|online-order-management|Ask the morning staff member to find yesterday's accepted test order and distinguish it from an incomplete attempt or request. Review item details, payment state and receiving information before choosing a next action. Include a second order type if the store uses specialist programs. This checks whether the person doing the work can make a confident decision without a developer narrating the screen.|The record should explain what is paid, what is promised and what remains. Document the escalation route when an order's state is unclear instead of encouraging staff to guess.
ecomm|order-status-history|Choose an order with several recorded transitions and ask a colleague to explain the sequence without relying on personal memory. Compare the current state with the available history and any customer message. Then discuss how a mistaken action would be reviewed through the supported process. This gives staff a factual basis for answering questions after a handover between shifts.|A useful history explains the saved actions and their context. Do not describe an unrecorded physical collection as proven simply because someone changed a status without the necessary operational checks.
ecomm|order-refunds|Use an authorized test order with more than one item and review a supported partial refund. Inspect the chosen item and amount before the action, then compare the order and payment records afterward. If the order belongs to a fundraiser or another program, inspect its related reporting too. This makes the exception part of the normal operating review rather than an afterthought.|The correction should remain tied to the original purchase and be explainable to staff and customer. External payment actions may need separate reconciliation before every related report agrees.
ecomm|inventory-connection-review|Select one mapped variant and inspect its source quantity, last successful refresh and storefront presentation. Then discuss the expected behaviour if the source stops updating. Include a product whose description comes from a catalogue while stock comes from a POS so the separate responsibilities are clear. A polished listing should never be the only evidence of a healthy inventory connection.|The team should know the authority, direction and cadence of the update. Keep unsupported historical or write-back assumptions out of the proposed operating process.
ecomm|read-only-pos-inventory-links|Draw the actual supported direction on a simple checklist: which fields are read from the source and which actions remain in the POS. Verify a sample mapped item and inspect a later update through the approved test process. Then ask what happens after a web order. This exposes an important distinction between reading source availability and writing a resulting adjustment back.|A read-only connection can be useful when its boundaries are understood. Confirm any separate order or adjustment pathway rather than promising a two-way relationship because two systems share a product identifier.
ecomm|product-activation-review|Take a complete imported product and walk through the remaining decisions before selling: identity, public information, availability, price, receiving options and store readiness. Include one item that is matched but still unsuitable for activation. Ask the responsible person to explain why one is approved and the other remains under review. This creates a manageable first range and a repeatable approval process.|The meaningful outcome is an approved sellable offer in the enabled store configuration. Imported, matched and published records should not be treated as interchangeable shortcuts to that decision.

crm|bloom-ai-assistant|Bring a specific task, such as understanding what information is missing before a seasonal campaign can be prepared. Ask Bloom using the account context available to it, then review the response and proposed action separately. If the answer refers to a customer, campaign or report, inspect that underlying record. A helpful conversation should make the next step clearer without replacing the store’s judgement about its business.|Staff should be able to distinguish an answer, a proposed action and a completed change. Review the actual result in the product before telling another person that the work is done.
crm|ai-campaign-drafting|Use a short approved brief for a workshop with a real date, price, location and public destination. Ask for a draft and compare each factual detail with the brief. Then edit the tone and remove any invented inclusion or urgency. Follow the supported route into a saved campaign only after the copy is useful. This keeps creative assistance connected to the operational record the team will eventually approve.|The useful result is a reviewed draft that accurately describes the offer. Confirm separately whether a campaign was saved, which audience is selected and whether any sending action has been authorized.
crm|marketing-calendar|Choose the next few weeks of real store activity and identify the marketing preparation each item needs. Review the calendar against available staff time and the public dates already promised. Include a campaign that remains only an idea and another with a confirmed schedule so the distinction is visible. Then ask the person running the program to explain the next piece of work they own.|A useful calendar supports decisions about preparation and timing. Keep the plan aligned with actual campaign and event records instead of treating every calendar item as a completed publication or confirmed send.
crm|marketing-content-library|Choose one available piece of seasonal material and compare it with the store’s actual products, local conditions and customer vocabulary. Adapt the message, check any factual guidance and add a destination that serves the invitation. Ask a knowledgeable colleague to review it before it becomes a campaign. The demonstration should show the work saved by a good starting point while keeping the store’s own judgement visible.|The final material should sound appropriate for the business and describe something it can actually offer. Reuse should shorten preparation without carrying old dates, unsupported claims or another region’s timing into the next message.
crm|crm-signup-forms|Prepare a controlled signup example with the intended purpose and required fields. Submit using contact details the team is authorized to test, then find the resulting record through the normal staff workflow. Inspect the saved preferences and the account or audience receiving the submission. Include a missing or invalid field so the reviewer sees how a visitor receives useful feedback before submission.|The form should create a record the team can locate and explain. Judge the full path, including destination and permission, rather than only the visual success state shown after clicking the button.
crm|email-preference-centre|Use a test subscriber who chooses an interest such as houseplants and later changes their email preference. Review the values saved after each action. Then examine the relevant audience or delivery logic to explain how the choice will affect a future message. This distinguishes a pleasant preference screen from an operational path that actually respects the recorded choice.|The team should be able to explain which campaigns use those preferences and which rules remain separate. An interest selection should never be presented as permission to send through an unrelated channel.
website|guided-plant-finder|Choose growing conditions a real shopper might know, such as the light in a garden and the space available. Answer the configured questions, review the matching public catalogue results and open a plant. Then return or reload to inspect the supported restoration behaviour. Include a combination that finds no useful match so staff can explain how the visitor continues browsing without mistaking an empty result for a complete botanical verdict.|The finder should reduce uncertainty using the actual configured attributes. A suggested match is a discovery aid; verify the listing, suitability information and available buying options separately.
website|ai-page-drafting|Prepare a short approved brief for a service the business really offers. Use the available generation workflow, then inspect the page’s structure, factual statements and proposed next action. Review any images and links separately before publication. Ask the staff member responsible for the content to identify which parts are ready and which still need editing. This makes the demonstration about a practical editorial handoff rather than the speed of generating a large volume of text.|A useful starting page should reduce blank-screen work while leaving approval clear. Keep invented dates, prices, qualifications or service promises out of the public version.
website|website-migration-review|Choose an older page containing useful text, several links and an embedded layout element. Compare the source with the migrated result and identify which information matters to the customer. Check the new heading structure, public destination and related links. Then inspect a page with a known exception so the review explains what remains manual rather than quietly treating the successful sample as proof that every old page moved correctly.|A useful migration preserves valuable information and makes remaining work visible. Keep the old source and an address mapping available until the important customer paths have been verified.
website|imported-image-review|Inspect an imported page after its images have finished loading. Check whether the photograph belongs with the content, whether the source is suitable for continued use and how the crop works on a phone. Include one broken reference and one plausible but unrelated fallback in the review. This exposes a page that technically has image data but still fails the visitor’s visual expectation.|The public page should show a relevant photograph with appropriate permission and a useful composition. Do not approve a migration solely because an image field or URL is populated.
website|site-doctor-website-checks|Start with a current finding and locate the exact page element it describes. Review the available safe correction and the effect it is intended to have before using the supported path in a demonstration environment. Reopen the public page and inspect the result, then review the updated finding state. Include an issue that needs a different kind of intervention so the tool’s scope remains understandable.|A useful check links evidence, a specific task and a verified result. A scan is a guide to review work, not a blanket certification that the website contains no other problems.
website|custom-domain-launch-planning|Review the domain’s current owner and the provider holding its records. Identify the website routing changes separately from mail and other service records, and agree the intended cutover. After the approved change, inspect the public domain and important addresses from a visitor’s perspective. The plan should also name who can correct an unexpected routing problem rather than relying on the person who happens to be available that day.|A dependable launch preserves services the business already uses and makes the new website reachable under the intended address. Keep an understandable record of what changed and which settings were deliberately left alone.
ecomm|seasonal-pre-sale-pages|Choose a small future collection and prepare its campaign settings in an authorized test store. Review the linked public page, including the explanation of later availability and the products shown in the campaign block. Change a permitted campaign detail and inspect how the presentation reflects the source record. This confirms that the campaign owns the commercial promise rather than a copied paragraph in page content becoming an independent source of truth.|The customer should understand the collection and future timing before ordering. Confirm store-specific release and checkout acceptance separately before opening a real campaign.
ecomm|pre-sale-product-options|Use a plant with two active sizes and choose each exact option for the demonstration campaign. Review the separate price and allocation attached to each offer, then follow one selection into the supported test basket and accepted order. Include an inactive option to explain why it should not become a new sellable offer. This makes the future purchase as specific as an ordinary item already in stock.|The campaign, customer and staff should identify the same size. Resolve ambiguous parent-product selections or missing variant setup before publishing the collection.
ecomm|future-stock-allocations|Set an approved future allocation in a test campaign and review how sold quantities and active checkout holds affect the remaining capacity. Discuss a proposed reduction below existing commitments so the rule is understandable before staff encounter it during a busy season. Compare that campaign quantity with the product’s ordinary on-hand stock. The two values serve different promises and should not be treated as interchangeable.|The owner should be able to explain what is promised and what remains available for new orders. Future supply needs its own approval; entering an allocation does not manufacture stock ready for immediate collection.
ecomm|pre-sale-pickup-promises|Write an expected arrival window and, where supported and confirmed, a separate pickup window with practical collection instructions. Review the customer acknowledgement in the authorized test flow, then inspect the details retained on an accepted order. Discuss a later campaign edit so staff can explain why the original customer promise must remain readable even when the public page changes for new shoppers.|The resulting record should preserve the terms actually accepted. Keep forecast arrival, confirmed collection arrangements and a later readiness update distinct in both staff training and customer wording.
ecomm|pre-sale-payment-review|Rehearse the configured full-payment-now path for a later-pickup campaign using an authorized test payment. Inspect the final amount, accepted order and customer-facing explanation together. Include the stated cancellation or refund policy in the review so the commercial promise is clear before the customer commits. Do not substitute a deposit example for a pathway the implemented campaign does not support.|The demonstration should make payment timing explicit and confirm the required store readiness. A locally rendered confirmation or queued message is not evidence that a real customer payment or email delivery has been verified.
ecomm|closing-pre-sale-campaigns|Use a test campaign containing an accepted order and review the supported close-sales action. Open the public page and staff order view afterward to confirm that new ordering has stopped while existing commitments remain understandable. Discuss the separate rules for unpublishing and reopening so staff do not use a destructive-seeming shortcut simply to hide a finished season.|The store should retain the information needed to serve every accepted order. Closing a sales window should not be confused with erasing its history or abandoning the agreed pickup process.

`;
export const deepDives = Object.fromEntries(rows.trim().split('\n').filter(Boolean).map(row => { const [product,slug,walkthrough,assessment]=row.split('|');return [`${product}/${slug}`,{walkthrough,assessment}]; }));
